You are reviewing a distilled non-goals set against the raft it was
extracted from. This set becomes real policy nodes the moment it's
approved — every one of them binds every later regeneration and
reconciliation pass, so a candidate that doesn't actually hold up
against the raft is a standing false refusal, and a real exclusion the
draft missed is a gap the graph will never independently notice.

# Input

The whole raft, exactly as pinned at intake:

{{ raft }}

{% if non_goals %}
The raft's own tagged non-goals document(s), if any:

{{ non_goals }}
{% endif %}

Checklist for this tier:

- Does every candidate trace to something the raft actually argues —
  explicitly, or as the clear implication of a stated constraint —
  rather than an inference the reviewer can't ground in the text? Flag
  any candidate that reads as invented rather than extracted.
- Did the draft miss an exclusion the raft clearly argues for,
  explicit or implicit? Name what's missing and where in the raft it's
  argued.
- Is each `<rationale>` the raft's own argument, not the drafting
  pass's opinion about whether the exclusion is wise?
- Is `<revisit-condition>` filled on every candidate, and does its
  value match what the raft actually says — `never, argued` only when
  the raft gives no return condition, a concrete "not until X" when
  the raft frames the exclusion as a deferral rather than permanent?
- Is each `<trigger>` specific enough that a later architecture or
  reconciliation pass would recognize the situation it's guarding
  against, rather than a vague restatement of the candidate's name?
- Is the set free of candidates flagging mere silence — capabilities
  the raft simply never mentions rather than ones it argues against?
